          %h1#bishops_constructive_mathematics Bishop’s constructive mathematics
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#overview"} Overview
              %li
                %a{:href => "#WhatItIs"} What is it?
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Triposes"} Relation to triposes
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Continuity"} Bishop’s notion of continuity
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#overview Overview
          %p
            In 1967,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Errett+Bishop"} Errett Bishop
            revived interest and practice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            with his book
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Foundations+of+Constructive+Analysis"} Foundations of Constructive Analysis
          %p
            While
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brouwer"} Brouwer
            had insisted that mathematics was primary to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionism"} intuitionists
            who followed him went more into intuitionistic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/philosophy"} philosophy
            than into mathematics as such. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Russian+constructivism"} Russian school
            of constructivism focussed on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/recursion+theory"} recursion theory
            and was far removed from modern
            = succeed "-style" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bourbaki"} Bourbaki
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analysis"} analysis
          %p
            Bishop's work was primarily in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analysis"} analysis
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorics"} combinatorics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            by his disciple
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fred+Richman"} Fred Richman
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            are so much
            %em more
            difficult constructively that Bishop did not even consider them; later authors have attacked the problem with tools such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/apartness+spaces"} apartness spaces
            and
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+topologies"} formal topologies
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Continuity"} below
          %p
            Bishop’s work is important not only for its historical role in reviving interest in constructive mathematics and belief in its feasibility, but because the framework for constructive mathematics that he laid out has played a central role in a great deal of later work. Known as
            %strong Bishop’s constructive mathematics
            and abbreviated
            %strong BISH
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %strong BCM
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+toposes"} elementary toposes
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#WhatItIs"} below
            %em is
            true. Thus, unless one really does want to work in the mathematics that Bishop himself did, it is better to work in “pure neutral” constructive mathematics and say so explicitly, while clearly mentioning any additional principles one may be assuming.
          %p
            This page will discuss Bishop’s constructive mathematics
            = succeed ";" do
              %em as Bishop did it
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            for “pure neutral” constructive mathematics that is valid in any topos. Unfortunately, the confusion surrounding BISH means that any individual paper claiming to use BISH must be read carefully to find out exactly what framework it
            %em is
            using.
          %h2#WhatItIs What is it?
          %p
            Bishop’s constructive mathematics is often described as “obtained from classical mathematics by removing the law of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            and the
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BridgesRichman"} BridgesRichman
          %p
            First of all, Bishop accepted the principle of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of many
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "#Richman00"} Richman
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZF"} ZF
            (without the axiom of choice).
          %p
            However, focusing attention on countable choice is misleading, because it may lead one to think that we could simply “remove that assumption” from BISH, and that proofs in BISH that don’t happen to invoke countable choice would be topos-valid. In fact it is not really possible to “remove countable choice” from BISH, because it is not an axiom but a
            = succeed "," do
              %em theorem
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentation+axiom"} presentation axiom
          %p
            The central point is the following: although the carriers of mathematical structure in BISH are, like in other mathematical frameworks, called
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            %em
              axiomatic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            but a
            = succeed "." do
              %em definitional set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            (and also some ways of using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            as a foundation, such as
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            %em defined
            in terms of more basic notions such as “construction” and “method”. (See
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bishop+set"} Bishop set
            %em prove
            that sets satisfy various properties, including countable choice. But Bishop directly uses “constructions” and “methods” throughout in addition to the “sets” that are defined from them, so his mathematics cannot be simply interpreted into an axiomatic set theory with certain assumptions added such as countable choice.
          %p
            Bishop did not formalize his foundations, and in particular did not specify rules for how “constructions” and “methods” should behave. However, many people believe that a fairly faithful reading is obtained by viewing “methods” as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            are classifiers for families of “constructions”. (Indeed, making this work was one of
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Per+Martin-Lof"} Per Martin-Lof
            %em logic
            is then the
            %em untruncated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"} propositions as types
            logic of this type theory, with for instance “or” interpreted as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum+type"} sum type
            and “there exists” interpreted as the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum+type"} dependent sum type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/explicit+mathematics"} explicit mathematics
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NuPrl"} NuPrl
            %a{:href => "http://www.nuprl.org/book/Overview.html"} here
            ). Alternatively, one can try to isolate the principles implied by Bishop’s definitional set theory to formulate an axiomatic set theory in which Bishop’s work can be formalized — one common proposal is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CZF"} CZF
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentation+axiom"} presentation axiom
            — but this is getting further from what Bishop actually did.)
          %p
            Thus, for instance, when Bishop says that a set is defined by specifying how to construct an element of it and how to say when two such elements are equal, we take this as meaning that a set is specified by a type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and an equality relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi Type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E : A \to A \to Type
            satisfying the laws of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            (a.k.a. a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/setoid"} setoid
            in the type theory). And whenever Bishop says “for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            there exists a
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo :
                        %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{x:A} \sum_{y:B}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            entails the existence of a function
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is “minimal” or “identity” or “Leibniz” (that is, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
            of the type theory). In particular, since this is true for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
          %p
            Now we can see the more basic problem with saying that BISH specializes to other theories such as topos-logic or ZF. It’s not just that BISH proves statements that “don’t hold” in these other theories; rather, the latter
            %em have
            no “constructions/methods” layer underneath, so there isn’t even any natural way to
            %em try
            to interpret BISH into them. (One might even argue that the same is true for the other flavors of constructivism that are generally believed to be specializations of BISH, such as Brouwer’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+mathematics"} intuitionistic mathematics
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Russian+constructivism"} Russian constructivism
            %em types
            in the underlying type theory; but in this case BISH is not talking about the same things as the other theory, and theorems of BISH do not specialize to the corresponding theorems of the other theory. (For classical mathematics
            %em with
            choice, though, it happens that this specialization is correct: every setoid is isomorphic to a set, namely the actual quotient of its equality equivalence relation.)
          %h2#Triposes Relation to triposes
          %p
            One of the most distinctive aspects of Bishop’s framework for mathematics is the notion that a “set” is something
            = succeed "," do
              %em equipped with an equality relation
          %p
            First we have to allow a more general “logic” than untruncated propositions-as-types, such as the topos logic of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobjects"} subobjects
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+hyperdoctrine"} first-order hyperdoctrine
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unique+choice"} function comprehension
            (unique choice) principle
            %em with respect to setoids
            (though it may with respect to the “natural” equality of objects of the topos). In fact it is nearly impossible for it to do so: if it does, then the morphisms in the base category of the hyperdoctrine satisfy the full axiom of choice! For if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a total relation from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            in such a hyperdoctrine, define a setoid equality on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times B
            by setting
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_1,y_1) = (x_2,y_2)
            iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1=x_2
            (in the minimal Leibniz sense) and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↔
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(x_1,y_1) \leftrightarrow R(x_2,y_2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            induces a total functional relation from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times B
            by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R'(x,(x',y)) = (x=x') \wedge R(x,y)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\to A\times B
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R'(x,f(x))
            then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1 f : A \to B
            satisfies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(x,\pi_1 f(x))
            and so is a choice function.
          %p
            Thus, to obtain function comprehension, which is a
            %em sine qua non
            for much mathematics, we have to essentially generalize the notion of “function” to be not just an operation (a term in the underlying type theory) that preserves equality, but a total functional relation. The “sets” in a hyperdoctrine, with the “functions” of this sort between them, are most studied in the case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triposes"} triposes
            and are called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em tripos-to-topos construction
          %p
            Thus, working with “sets” in tripos logic (defined there as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+equivalence+relations"} partial equivalence relations
          %h2#Continuity Bishop’s notion of continuity
          %p
            Bishop rejected the usual notion of pointwise
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
            (and more generally
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            to
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Waaldijk05"} Waaldijk05
            %em FCA
            and
            %em CA
            use different definitions, which can be reconciled only using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fan+theorem"} fan theorem
            (which Bishop rejected). There is a similar problem with the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+compact+space"} locally compact space
            %em any
            notion of “kontinuous” function between subsets of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            which is (1) closed under composition, (2) specializes on closed intervals to uniformly continuous functions, and (3) includes
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)= \frac{1}{x}
          %p
            A more modern perspective is to use
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
            theory (or the predicative variant
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+topologies"} formal topologies
            %a{:href => "#Palmgren03"} Palmgren
            shows that the continuous endomaps of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale+of+real+numbers"} locale of real numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            coincide with Bishop’s locally uniformly continuous functions, whereas for spaces other than
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            itself locale maps are better-behaved than Bishops’: in particular, they are of course closed under composition, and they contain inversion (suitably interpreted). Similarly, locally compact spaces and measure theory are arguably better done with locales than spaces. In particular, see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localic+completion"} localic completion
            and the references below.
          %p
            Alternatively, one can consider sheaves over the localic unit interval to model the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fan+theorem"} fan theorem
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+truth"} continuous truth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/choice+sequences"} choice sequences
            to justify the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fan+theorem"} fan theorem
          %h2#references References
          %p Books about or using Bishop’s constructive mathematics include:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Errett+Bishop"}> Errett Bishop
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Foundations+of+Constructive+Analysis"} Foundations of Constructive Analysis
                (1967), the work which made him famous in the foundations of mathematics circles;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Errett+Bishop"} Errett Bishop
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henry+Cheng"} Henry Cheng
                %em Constructive Measure Theory
                (1972), featuring a better
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
                than in
                = succeed ";" do
                  %em FCA
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Errett+Bishop"} Errett Bishop
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Douglas+Bridges"} Douglas Bridges
                %em Constructive Analysis
                (1985), a revised version of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em FCA
                = succeed "." do
                  %em CMT
            %li
              %p
                %span.newWikiWord>
                  Ray Mines
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Ray+Mines"} ?
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fred+Richman"} Fred Richman
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wim+Ruitenberg"} Wim Ruitenberg
                %em A course in constructive algebra
                (1988)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Douglas+Bridges"} Douglas Bridges
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Luminiţa Vîţă
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Lumini%C5%A3a+V%C3%AE%C5%A3%C4%83"} ?
                %em Apartness and Uniformity: A Constructive Development
                (2011)
            %li#BridgesRichman
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Douglas+Bridges"} Douglas Bridges
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fred+Richman"} Fred Richman
                %em Varieties of constructive mathematics
                (1987)
            %li#BridgesRichman
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Douglas+Bridges"}> Douglas Bridges
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hajime+Ishihara"} Hajime Ishihara
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Rathjen"} Michael Rathjen
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Helmut+Schwichtenberg"} Helmut Schwichtenberg
                %em Handbook of Constructive Mathematics
                (2023)
          %p Other papers mentioned above:
          %ul#Richman00
            %li#Waaldijk05
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Franka+Waaldijk"}> Franka Waaldijk
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the foundations of constructive mathematics - especially in relation to the theory of continuous functions
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s10699-004-3065-z"} doi:10.1007/s10699-004-3065-z
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.fwaaldijk.nl/foundations%20of%20constructive%20mathematics.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.fwaaldijk.nl/mathematics.html"} website
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fred+Richman"}> Fred Richman
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Constructive Mathematics without Choice
              %p
                in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Reuniting the Antipodes – Constructive and Nonstandard Views of the Continuum
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 306
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-015-9757-9_17"} doi:10.1007/978-94-015-9757-9_17
          %p For more about the localic approach, see:
          %ul#Palmgren03
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Erik+Palmgren"}> Erik Palmgren
              ,
              = succeed "." do
                %em Continuity on the real line and in formal spaces
              %a{:href => "http://www2.math.uu.se/~palmgren/crealform-rvd2.pdf"} link
          %ul#Palmgren05
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Erik+Palmgren"}> Erik Palmgren
              ,
              %em From intuitionistic to point-free topology
              (2005) U.U.D.M. Report 2005:47.
              %a{:href => "http://www2.math.uu.se/~palmgren/homotopy.pdf"} link
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Erik+Palmgren"}> Erik Palmgren
                ,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em A constructive and functorial embedding of locally compact metric spaces into locales
              %p (2007), 1854–1880.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Erik+Palmgren"}> Erik Palmgren
                ,
                = succeed "," do
